From a97e6c772ce56aa1ee694d4a493a6fbb6db21940 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 22:19:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 78/82] util/disk_cache: a Horizon store that fails to open
 leaves no queue behind
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 7 item 1 (Mesa shader disk cache on Horizon)
Why: disk_cache_type_create() on Horizon opens the store after the
 driver keys blob exists, i.e. after disk_cache_init_queue() has already
 started the put queue. When the open fails it finishes and destroys
 the queue and marks the cache path_init_failed — but util_queue_destroy
 frees `jobs` and `threads` and leaves the pointers in place, and
 util_queue_is_initialized() is `queue->threads != NULL`. So the cache
 object returned to NVK still looks initialised to everything that asks:
 disk_cache_put() allocates and copies a job for every pipeline blob and
 hands it to a queue with no threads (util_queue_add_job_locked returns
 without running the cleanup when num_threads is 0), leaking one copy of
 every pipeline serialised for the life of the instance; and
 disk_cache_destroy() finishes and destroys the queue a second time —
 cnd_destroy and mtx_destroy on destroyed objects and free() of both
 arrays again — on a games console with no allocator hardening.
 A failed open is an ordinary event here: a read-only or full card, or
 the cache path present as a directory, all make horizon_gpu_blob_cache_
 open() return an error and the log say "cache disabled".
Evidence: u_queue.c:
   void util_queue_destroy(struct util_queue *queue) {
      ... free(queue->jobs); free(queue->threads); }
   static inline bool util_queue_is_initialized(struct util_queue *queue)
   { return queue->threads != NULL; }
 and util_queue_init's own failure path, which is what this now copies:
   /* also util_queue_is_initialized can be used to check for success */
   memset(queue, 0, sizeof(*queue));
 disk_cache.c: disk_cache_put() and disk_cache_destroy() both gate on
 util_queue_is_initialized() and not on path_init_failed.
 Class X: cross-built with the series; the path needs a card that
 refuses the open to run, and has not been run on a console.
Upstream: no — it fixes the Horizon-only branch 0046 added.
---
 src/util/disk_cache.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/util/disk_cache.c b/src/util/disk_cache.c
index 0e251f4..ae49279 100644
--- a/src/util/disk_cache.c
+++ b/src/util/disk_cache.c
@@ -216,6 +216,14 @@ disk_cache_type_create(const char *gpu_name,
        !disk_cache_horizon_open(cache)) {
       util_queue_finish(&cache->cache_queue);
       util_queue_destroy(&cache->cache_queue);
+      /* util_queue_destroy frees the arrays and leaves the pointers, and
+       * util_queue_is_initialized() is `threads != NULL` — so without
+       * this the cache object walks out of here looking initialised:
+       * disk_cache_put() keeps allocating jobs for a queue that has no
+       * threads, and disk_cache_destroy() finishes and destroys it a
+       * second time. This is what util_queue_init() itself does on its
+       * own failure path (u_queue.c). */
+      memset(&cache->cache_queue, 0, sizeof(cache->cache_queue));
       cache->path_init_failed = true;
       cache->type = DISK_CACHE_NONE;
    }
